{"author":{"id":"8d2e0405f08ac6c481f912544d6d5c0f58b2dc14a7e51ad4d499ee7a34567943"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1795700025,"nonce":"3KJToVcRriSB23oUCJVvqOYy0YU=","title":"JAX_PLATFORMS=cuda without cpu fails in assembly with a bare \"Unknown backend cpu\"","message":"Running jNO on a GPU with `JAX_PLATFORMS=cuda` (no `cpu` entry) makes FEM solves and many tests fail with `RuntimeError: Unknown backend cpu. Available backends are ['cuda']`.\n\n**What goes wrong.** Assembly deliberately runs on the host: `_host_assembly_scope` in jno/_fem.py calls `jax.devices(\"cpu\")`. With the CPU platform absent from `JAX_PLATFORMS`, JAX raises instead of returning an empty list, so the guard right after it (`return ... if hosts else contextlib.nullcontext()`) can never take the empty branch. The same happens in `jno/core.py` (the CPU-device helper, line ~246), whose own check (\"No CPU JAX device found. Set JAX_PLATFORMS=cuda,cpu\") is also unreachable for the same reason, so its helpful message never shows. Test failures reported the same error from pure_callback paths; I did not trace those separately.\n\n**Reproduce.**\n```\nJAX_PLATFORMS=cuda python -c \"import jax; jax.devices('cpu')\"\n```\nprints `RuntimeError: Unknown backend cpu. Available backends are ['cuda']`. Any `jno.fem(...).solve()` with `JAX_PLATFORMS=cuda` reaches it.\n\n**Expected.** Either it works (assemble on the device when no host backend exists, which the code already intends via the `nullcontext` branch) or it fails once, early, with the message that tells the user to set `JAX_PLATFORMS=cuda,cpu`. A bare JAX backend error from deep inside assembly names neither jNO nor the remedy.\n\n**Where.** jno/_fem.py `_host_assembly_scope` (the `jax.devices(\"cpu\")` call); jno/core.py CPU-device helper (same call); pure_callback and host-staging paths that assume a CPU backend.\n\n**Workaround.** Set `JAX_PLATFORMS=cuda,cpu` (as the docs and the helper's message say), or leave the variable unset.\n\n**Direction.** Wrap the lookup in a small helper that catches the RuntimeError and returns `[]`, used by both places; then `_host_assembly_scope` falls back to the device and `core` raises its own clear message. Add a test that runs with the platform list restricted to the accelerator (a subprocess), so the fallback is exercised.","files":null}]}